1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,121
Imagine four bad mornings.

2
00:00:02,671 --> 00:00:04,578
One: someone deletes a release,

3
00:00:04,578 --> 00:00:06,854
and every build that needs it breaks.

4
00:00:06,854 --> 00:00:08,207
Two: the disk is full,

5
00:00:08,207 --> 00:00:12,143
and half of it is old snapshots that nobody will ever use again.

6
00:00:12,693 --> 00:00:16,770
Three: the jar on a server is not the jar your pipeline built,

7
00:00:16,770 --> 00:00:18,085
and nobody can tell.

8
00:00:18,085 --> 00:00:19,531
Four: the server dies,

9
00:00:19,531 --> 00:00:21,504
and everything on it dies too.

10
00:00:22,054 --> 00:00:23,794
A repository is a promise.

11
00:00:23,794 --> 00:00:25,267
Last time we made one.

12
00:00:25,267 --> 00:00:28,479
Today we make the promise keep itself: prove it,

13
00:00:28,479 --> 00:00:29,216
protect it,

14
00:00:29,216 --> 00:00:29,818
clean it,

15
00:00:29,818 --> 00:00:30,822
and back it up.

16
00:00:31,372 --> 00:00:35,937
All of it live, on the same real Nexus, with the same Cloud Cart library.

17
00:00:37,487 --> 00:00:38,916
Here is what to expect.

18
00:00:39,466 --> 00:00:40,480
One: two ideas,

19
00:00:40,480 --> 00:00:41,291
in pictures.

20
00:00:41,291 --> 00:00:43,725
Immutable does not mean untouchable.

21
00:00:43,725 --> 00:00:46,293
And cleaning up is a job in two steps.

22
00:00:46,843 --> 00:00:48,062
Two: the live work,

23
00:00:48,062 --> 00:00:48,960
in four parts.

24
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:50,820
Prove where a file came from.

25
00:00:50,820 --> 00:00:52,745
Take away the power to delete.

26
00:00:52,745 --> 00:00:54,734
Keep only the newest snapshots.

27
00:00:54,734 --> 00:00:55,888
And take a backup.

28
00:00:56,438 --> 00:00:58,150
Three: a proof for each.

29
00:00:58,150 --> 00:01:00,931
A checksum that catches a changed file.

30
00:01:00,931 --> 00:01:02,215
A 403 on a delete.

31
00:01:02,215 --> 00:01:04,568
A repository that really shrinks.

32
00:01:04,568 --> 00:01:06,422
And a backup file on disk.

33
00:01:06,972 --> 00:01:11,270
This lesson covers two objectives of the Nexus domain: immutability,

34
00:01:11,270 --> 00:01:12,850
retention and provenance,

35
00:01:12,850 --> 00:01:13,924
then permissions,

36
00:01:13,924 --> 00:01:15,378
backups and monitoring.

37
00:01:16,928 --> 00:01:20,189
First idea: immutable does not mean untouchable.

38
00:01:20,189 --> 00:01:20,868
Last time,

39
00:01:20,868 --> 00:01:23,382
Nexus refused to overwrite a release.

40
00:01:23,382 --> 00:01:25,420
That is the deployment policy,

41
00:01:25,420 --> 00:01:28,341
and it protects a file from being replaced.

42
00:01:28,891 --> 00:01:32,418
But a person with the delete privilege can still remove it.

43
00:01:32,418 --> 00:01:33,614
And once it is gone,

44
00:01:33,614 --> 00:01:37,559
someone can publish a different jar under the same version number.

45
00:01:37,559 --> 00:01:40,070
The deployment policy never saw it coming.

46
00:01:40,620 --> 00:01:41,752
So we add layers.

47
00:01:41,752 --> 00:01:42,418
Layer one,

48
00:01:42,418 --> 00:01:44,816
the deployment policy: no overwrite.

49
00:01:44,816 --> 00:01:45,483
Layer two,

50
00:01:45,483 --> 00:01:48,613
least privilege: the deployer can add and read,

51
00:01:48,613 --> 00:01:49,612
but not delete.

52
00:01:49,612 --> 00:01:50,412
Layer three,

53
00:01:50,412 --> 00:01:52,943
provenance: every file has a checksum,

54
00:01:52,943 --> 00:01:55,074
and a record of who uploaded it,

55
00:01:55,074 --> 00:01:55,807
from where,

56
00:01:55,807 --> 00:01:56,407
and when.

57
00:01:56,957 --> 00:01:59,078
Provenance does not stop a change.

58
00:01:59,078 --> 00:02:00,700
It makes a change visible.

59
00:02:00,700 --> 00:02:03,257
A checksum is a fingerprint of the bytes.

60
00:02:03,257 --> 00:02:04,255
Change one byte,

61
00:02:04,255 --> 00:02:06,002
and the fingerprint changes.

62
00:02:06,552 --> 00:02:08,451
So: prevent, restrict, prove.

63
00:02:10,001 --> 00:02:12,743
Second idea: cleaning up takes two steps.

64
00:02:12,743 --> 00:02:14,749
When Nexus deletes a snapshot,

65
00:02:14,749 --> 00:02:17,424
it does not free the disk straight away.

66
00:02:17,424 --> 00:02:19,764
It only marks the files as deleted.

67
00:02:19,764 --> 00:02:20,700
A second task,

68
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:22,439
called compact blob store,

69
00:02:22,439 --> 00:02:23,910
removes them for real.

70
00:02:24,460 --> 00:02:26,217
What to delete is a policy.

71
00:02:26,217 --> 00:02:27,127
For snapshots,

72
00:02:27,127 --> 00:02:30,444
a simple and safe rule: keep the newest few builds,

73
00:02:30,444 --> 00:02:31,745
and let the rest go.

74
00:02:31,745 --> 00:02:33,241
Releases are different.

75
00:02:33,241 --> 00:02:36,428
A release is deleted only when a person means it.

76
00:02:36,978 --> 00:02:39,451
Third idea: a backup has two halves.

77
00:02:39,451 --> 00:02:42,405
The database holds the list of what exists.

78
00:02:42,405 --> 00:02:44,535
The blob store holds the bytes.

79
00:02:44,535 --> 00:02:45,634
Lose either one,

80
00:02:45,634 --> 00:02:47,626
and the repository is broken.

81
00:02:47,626 --> 00:02:50,305
So the backup takes the database first,

82
00:02:50,305 --> 00:02:51,336
then the blobs.

83
00:02:51,886 --> 00:02:54,702
And a healthy server is watched: free space,

84
00:02:54,702 --> 00:02:55,726
blob store size,

85
00:02:55,726 --> 00:02:57,134
and the health checks.

86
00:02:57,134 --> 00:02:58,606
So: two steps to clean,

87
00:02:58,606 --> 00:03:00,014
two halves to back up.

88
00:03:01,564 --> 00:03:05,288
We continue from the end of the last lesson: the same Nexus,

89
00:03:05,288 --> 00:03:06,467
signed in as admin,

90
00:03:06,467 --> 00:03:09,880
with cart-core published as a release and as snapshots.

91
00:03:09,880 --> 00:03:11,432
We start with provenance.

92
00:03:11,432 --> 00:03:14,535
How do we know that this file is the one we built?

93
00:03:15,085 --> 00:03:17,922
In Browse, we open the releases repository.

94
00:03:22,272 --> 00:03:24,683
We expand the tree, one level at a time.

95
00:03:25,233 --> 00:03:26,193
First, com.

96
00:03:29,343 --> 00:03:30,601
Then, Cloud Cart.

97
00:03:33,451 --> 00:03:34,646
Then, cart-core.

98
00:03:37,596 --> 00:03:40,455
And finally, the version, 1.0.0.

99
00:03:44,005 --> 00:03:45,690
These are the files of our release.

100
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:48,011
The jar is the one that matters.

101
00:03:48,561 --> 00:03:49,602
We click the jar.

102
00:03:49,602 --> 00:03:52,297
The panel on the right is the file's record.

103
00:03:52,297 --> 00:03:53,951
Here are its path and size,

104
00:03:53,951 --> 00:03:55,421
and when it was created.

105
00:03:55,421 --> 00:03:58,791
And here is the part we care about today: the uploader,

106
00:03:58,791 --> 00:03:59,648
cart-deployer,

107
00:03:59,648 --> 00:04:01,425
and the address it came from.

108
00:04:01,975 --> 00:04:03,127
We scroll back up.

109
00:04:04,677 --> 00:04:07,407
Next to Summary is a tab called Attributes.

110
00:04:11,757 --> 00:04:17,411
It holds the file's checksums: SHA-1, SHA-256, and MD5.

111
00:04:17,961 --> 00:04:20,316
A checksum is a fingerprint of the bytes,

112
00:04:20,316 --> 00:04:23,187
and Nexus computed it the moment the file arrived.

113
00:04:23,737 --> 00:04:25,542
Now we check it ourselves.

114
00:04:25,542 --> 00:04:26,653
In the terminal,

115
00:04:26,653 --> 00:04:29,708
two small helpers I defined for this lesson.

116
00:04:29,708 --> 00:04:31,999
REL is the folder of the release.

117
00:04:31,999 --> 00:04:33,040
And nx is curl,

118
00:04:33,040 --> 00:04:36,303
with the deployer's credentials already inside.

119
00:04:36,853 --> 00:04:37,261
First,

120
00:04:37,261 --> 00:04:40,463
we ask Nexus for the recorded SHA-1 of the jar.

121
00:04:40,463 --> 00:04:43,052
This is the fingerprint it wrote down,

122
00:04:43,052 --> 00:04:44,959
on the day the file arrived.

123
00:04:45,509 --> 00:04:48,475
Now we download the jar, to a folder of our own.

124
00:04:51,625 --> 00:04:53,779
And we compute the SHA-1 ourselves.

125
00:04:54,329 --> 00:04:55,865
The same forty characters.

126
00:04:56,415 --> 00:04:59,658
The file we hold is exactly the file that was uploaded.

127
00:05:00,208 --> 00:05:01,844
What if someone changes it?

128
00:05:01,844 --> 00:05:02,814
We copy the jar,

129
00:05:02,814 --> 00:05:04,208
add a single character,

130
00:05:04,208 --> 00:05:05,299
and compute again.

131
00:05:05,299 --> 00:05:06,572
Completely different.

132
00:05:06,572 --> 00:05:07,602
One byte changed,

133
00:05:07,602 --> 00:05:09,360
and the fingerprint tells us.

134
00:05:09,910 --> 00:05:12,234
Provenance tells us a file changed.

135
00:05:12,234 --> 00:05:14,956
It does not stop anyone from deleting it.

136
00:05:14,956 --> 00:05:15,156
So,

137
00:05:15,156 --> 00:05:16,749
second: least privilege.

138
00:05:16,749 --> 00:05:21,463
Last lesson the deployer received every action on our two repositories,

139
00:05:21,463 --> 00:05:23,057
to keep the setup short.

140
00:05:23,057 --> 00:05:26,443
Today we take back the one it never needed: delete.

141
00:05:26,993 --> 00:05:28,103
We open Roles.

142
00:05:33,953 --> 00:05:36,043
Then, we open the cart-deployer role.

143
00:05:39,793 --> 00:05:42,460
Under Privileges, we click Edit privileges.

144
00:05:45,010 --> 00:05:47,594
We filter by the releases repository.

145
00:05:47,594 --> 00:05:48,571
The first row,

146
00:05:48,571 --> 00:05:49,409
with a star,

147
00:05:49,409 --> 00:05:50,736
means every action.

148
00:05:50,736 --> 00:05:51,644
We untick it.

149
00:05:51,644 --> 00:05:54,786
Then we tick only what a deployer needs: add,

150
00:05:54,786 --> 00:05:56,183
to upload new files.

151
00:05:56,183 --> 00:05:57,300
Browse and read,

152
00:05:57,300 --> 00:05:58,837
to see and fetch them.

153
00:05:58,837 --> 00:05:59,465
And edit,

154
00:05:59,465 --> 00:06:03,236
because Maven updates a metadata file on every deploy.

155
00:06:03,236 --> 00:06:04,423
Delete stays off.

156
00:06:04,973 --> 00:06:07,341
The same for the snapshots repository.

157
00:06:12,091 --> 00:06:13,585
We filter by its name.

158
00:06:14,135 --> 00:06:15,329
We untick the star.

159
00:06:16,979 --> 00:06:18,131
Then, we tick add.

160
00:06:19,781 --> 00:06:20,720
Then, browse.

161
00:06:23,870 --> 00:06:24,851
Then, edit.

162
00:06:25,401 --> 00:06:26,191
And read.

163
00:06:28,241 --> 00:06:33,766
Ten privileges in total: four on each of our repositories, and two on the public group.

164
00:06:34,316 --> 00:06:35,255
We confirm.

165
00:06:39,605 --> 00:06:41,034
Then, we save the role.

166
00:06:45,384 --> 00:06:46,355
Now the proof.

167
00:06:46,355 --> 00:06:47,464
As the deployer,

168
00:06:47,464 --> 00:06:49,752
we try to delete the release jar.

169
00:06:49,752 --> 00:06:52,456
The helper prints only the status code.

170
00:06:52,456 --> 00:06:53,496
403: forbidden.

171
00:06:53,496 --> 00:06:56,547
The deployer can no longer remove a release,

172
00:06:56,547 --> 00:06:57,725
even by accident.

173
00:06:57,725 --> 00:06:59,875
And if its password ever leaks,

174
00:06:59,875 --> 00:07:02,579
nobody can use it to erase our history.

175
00:07:03,129 --> 00:07:05,657
Does the deployer still do its job?

176
00:07:05,657 --> 00:07:07,534
We go back to the project,

177
00:07:07,534 --> 00:07:09,629
and deploy the next snapshot,

178
00:07:09,629 --> 00:07:10,062
1.1.0.

179
00:07:10,062 --> 00:07:10,712
It works.

180
00:07:10,712 --> 00:07:11,001
Add,

181
00:07:11,001 --> 00:07:14,179
edit and read are all that a deployer needs.

182
00:07:18,529 --> 00:07:18,931
Third,

183
00:07:18,931 --> 00:07:19,601
retention.

184
00:07:19,601 --> 00:07:20,808
Snapshots pile up.

185
00:07:20,808 --> 00:07:22,818
Every deploy adds a new build,

186
00:07:22,818 --> 00:07:26,236
and nobody ever asks for build one from last month.

187
00:07:26,236 --> 00:07:28,045
To have something to clean,

188
00:07:28,045 --> 00:07:29,452
we deploy twice more.

189
00:07:48,502 --> 00:07:53,541
The search API lists every snapshot build in the repository.

190
00:07:53,541 --> 00:07:53,961
Five.

191
00:07:53,961 --> 00:07:55,221
Two from 1.0.0,

192
00:07:55,221 --> 00:07:57,405
which is already released,

193
00:07:57,405 --> 00:07:59,169
and three from 1.1.0.

194
00:07:59,719 --> 00:08:01,041
Now the cleaning job.

195
00:08:07,491 --> 00:08:10,051
Settings, System, Tasks, and Create task.

196
00:08:13,101 --> 00:08:14,595
There are many task types.

197
00:08:15,145 --> 00:08:17,619
The one we want is Maven, delete snapshot.

198
00:08:22,369 --> 00:08:26,956
We name it trim-snapshots, and choose the repository: cloudcart-snapshots.

199
00:08:30,906 --> 00:08:32,869
Minimum versions retained: two.

200
00:08:34,819 --> 00:08:37,037
We keep the newest two builds, and no more.

201
00:08:37,587 --> 00:08:41,065
Retention days: zero, because we do not need an age rule.

202
00:08:41,615 --> 00:08:43,492
And Remove if released: on.

203
00:08:44,042 --> 00:08:48,799
A snapshot whose release exists, like 1.0.0, has done its job.

204
00:08:49,349 --> 00:08:52,315
The schedule says Manual, so we decide when it runs.

205
00:08:59,665 --> 00:09:01,926
In real life, you would pick daily, at night.

206
00:09:02,476 --> 00:09:03,820
We click Create task.

207
00:09:08,770 --> 00:09:09,922
Then, we open it.

208
00:09:15,272 --> 00:09:16,552
And we click Run now.

209
00:09:19,802 --> 00:09:22,554
The task runs, and it finishes in a few seconds.

210
00:09:27,104 --> 00:09:29,387
We ask the search API again.

211
00:09:29,387 --> 00:09:31,507
The 1.0.0 builds are gone,

212
00:09:31,507 --> 00:09:34,197
because that version is released.

213
00:09:34,197 --> 00:09:35,420
And from 1.1.0,

214
00:09:35,420 --> 00:09:37,621
only the newest two remain.

215
00:09:38,171 --> 00:09:40,254
But the disk has not changed yet.

216
00:09:40,254 --> 00:09:41,200
In Blob Stores,

217
00:09:41,200 --> 00:09:43,409
the default store has a blob count.

218
00:09:43,409 --> 00:09:44,166
Remember it.

219
00:09:47,116 --> 00:09:49,377
Those deleted files are only marked.

220
00:09:54,527 --> 00:09:58,047
We create the second task: Admin, compact blob store.

221
00:10:00,597 --> 00:10:02,219
We name it compact-default.

222
00:10:07,769 --> 00:10:09,305
We choose the default store.

223
00:10:12,955 --> 00:10:14,064
And we create it.

224
00:10:22,514 --> 00:10:23,431
We open it.

225
00:10:29,781 --> 00:10:30,933
We click Run now.

226
00:10:34,283 --> 00:10:37,377
The task runs, and the blobs are removed from the disk.

227
00:10:41,627 --> 00:10:44,123
Back in Blob Stores: the count has dropped.

228
00:10:49,773 --> 00:10:51,287
The space is really free.

229
00:10:51,837 --> 00:10:53,949
One more tool: cleanup policies.

230
00:10:54,499 --> 00:10:55,994
They work for any format,

231
00:10:55,994 --> 00:10:56,831
on a schedule,

232
00:10:56,831 --> 00:11:00,238
and they are perfect for proxy caches that grow and grow.

233
00:11:00,788 --> 00:11:02,388
We open Cleanup Policies.

234
00:11:08,138 --> 00:11:09,333
And we create one.

235
00:11:12,983 --> 00:11:14,455
A name: stale-cache.

236
00:11:16,705 --> 00:11:18,177
The format: Maven.

237
00:11:23,327 --> 00:11:24,500
And a criterion.

238
00:11:25,050 --> 00:11:27,247
The criterion is Component usage.

239
00:11:32,797 --> 00:11:35,827
We remove what nobody has downloaded in sixty days.

240
00:11:36,377 --> 00:11:40,473
Before anything is deleted, the preview shows what the policy would remove.

241
00:11:41,823 --> 00:11:43,380
We choose maven-central.

242
00:11:47,130 --> 00:11:50,053
Nothing matches yet, because our cache is brand new.

243
00:11:50,603 --> 00:11:51,925
We save the policy.

244
00:11:57,575 --> 00:11:58,663
And we attach it.

245
00:11:59,213 --> 00:12:02,008
We open the maven-central repository.

246
00:12:06,758 --> 00:12:09,211
In the Cleanup section, we find our policy.

247
00:12:15,161 --> 00:12:16,441
We move it to Applied.

248
00:12:20,791 --> 00:12:21,751
And we save.

249
00:12:27,301 --> 00:12:30,245
From now on, the nightly cleanup service does the work.

250
00:12:30,795 --> 00:12:32,331
Fourth, the server itself.

251
00:12:37,781 --> 00:12:40,597
In Nexus, the database is backed up by a task.

252
00:12:41,147 --> 00:12:44,390
We create one: Admin, backup H2 database.

253
00:12:47,240 --> 00:12:49,203
We name it nightly-db-backup.

254
00:12:54,453 --> 00:12:56,351
And we give it a folder on the server.

255
00:12:59,401 --> 00:13:01,193
We scroll down, and continue.

256
00:13:07,243 --> 00:13:08,758
Then, we create the task.

257
00:13:13,608 --> 00:13:15,869
In real life, you would run it every night.

258
00:13:16,419 --> 00:13:17,571
We open the task.

259
00:13:22,821 --> 00:13:23,973
Here is its page.

260
00:13:24,523 --> 00:13:25,675
We click Run now.

261
00:13:29,025 --> 00:13:30,092
And we confirm.

262
00:13:36,342 --> 00:13:38,113
The backup runs in a few seconds.

263
00:13:38,663 --> 00:13:39,997
And here is the proof,

264
00:13:39,997 --> 00:13:41,816
in the terminal: one zip file,

265
00:13:41,816 --> 00:13:42,907
with today's date,

266
00:13:42,907 --> 00:13:44,180
in the backup folder.

267
00:13:44,180 --> 00:13:45,575
That is the first half.

268
00:13:46,125 --> 00:13:48,309
The second half is the blob store.

269
00:13:48,309 --> 00:13:50,171
It is a plain folder on disk,

270
00:13:50,171 --> 00:13:52,484
and a backup copies that folder too.

271
00:13:52,484 --> 00:13:53,704
The database first,

272
00:13:53,704 --> 00:13:54,668
then the blobs,

273
00:13:54,668 --> 00:13:57,943
so that every file the database knows about exists.

274
00:13:58,493 --> 00:13:59,539
Now, watching.

275
00:14:06,489 --> 00:14:09,134
In Blob Stores, we open the default store.

276
00:14:12,284 --> 00:14:13,905
And we enable a soft quota.

277
00:14:16,355 --> 00:14:17,977
The type: Space remaining.

278
00:14:21,827 --> 00:14:23,853
The limit: one thousand megabytes.

279
00:14:25,703 --> 00:14:29,223
Below that, Nexus raises an alert, before the disk is full.

280
00:14:32,573 --> 00:14:33,107
Settings,

281
00:14:33,107 --> 00:14:33,936
Metric health,

282
00:14:33,936 --> 00:14:35,773
shows the checks of the server,

283
00:14:35,773 --> 00:14:36,425
one by one,

284
00:14:36,425 --> 00:14:38,973
including the blob store quota we just set.

285
00:14:41,723 --> 00:14:43,419
And for a load balancer,

286
00:14:43,419 --> 00:14:44,903
or a monitoring tool,

287
00:14:44,903 --> 00:14:48,224
there is a status endpoint that needs no login.

288
00:14:48,224 --> 00:14:49,425
200 means: ready.

289
00:14:49,975 --> 00:14:52,122
That is a trustworthy repository.

290
00:14:52,122 --> 00:14:53,748
Every file can be proved,

291
00:14:53,748 --> 00:14:56,546
nobody holds more power than the job needs,

292
00:14:56,546 --> 00:14:58,563
old builds clean themselves up,

293
00:14:58,563 --> 00:15:00,449
and the server can come back.

294
00:15:00,999 --> 00:15:02,706
Now, the mistakes to avoid.

295
00:15:04,256 --> 00:15:05,707
Four mistakes to avoid.

296
00:15:06,257 --> 00:15:09,497
One: believing that no overwrite means no loss.

297
00:15:09,497 --> 00:15:12,943
A user with delete can still empty the repository.

298
00:15:12,943 --> 00:15:14,253
Give deployers add,

299
00:15:14,253 --> 00:15:15,218
edit and read,

300
00:15:15,218 --> 00:15:16,390
and nothing more.

301
00:15:16,940 --> 00:15:20,250
Two: deleting snapshots and forgetting to compact.

302
00:15:20,250 --> 00:15:21,574
The disk stays full,

303
00:15:21,574 --> 00:15:23,361
and nobody understands why.

304
00:15:23,911 --> 00:15:26,120
Three: backing up only the database.

305
00:15:26,120 --> 00:15:27,164
The list is safe,

306
00:15:27,164 --> 00:15:29,373
but the files it points to are gone.

307
00:15:29,923 --> 00:15:32,450
Four: a backup that was never restored,

308
00:15:32,450 --> 00:15:34,458
or a quota that nobody watches.

309
00:15:34,458 --> 00:15:35,560
Test the restore,

310
00:15:35,560 --> 00:15:36,856
and read the alerts.

311
00:15:38,406 --> 00:15:40,646
Your turn, on your own machine, in a lab.

312
00:15:41,196 --> 00:15:43,394
One: open the jar of your release,

313
00:15:43,394 --> 00:15:47,596
and compare its SHA-1 in Nexus with the one you compute yourself.

314
00:15:48,146 --> 00:15:53,031
Two: narrow your deployer to add, browse, edit and read, and prove the 403.

315
00:15:53,581 --> 00:15:55,535
Three: deploy a few snapshots,

316
00:15:55,535 --> 00:15:57,618
create the delete snapshot task,

317
00:15:57,618 --> 00:15:58,074
run it,

318
00:15:58,074 --> 00:15:59,832
and compact the blob store.

319
00:16:00,382 --> 00:16:04,030
Four: schedule a database backup, run it, and find the file.

320
00:16:04,580 --> 00:16:06,308
Pause the video, and do it now.

321
00:16:21,858 --> 00:16:22,733
Quick check.

322
00:16:23,283 --> 00:16:25,864
What does a checksum prove, and what does it not stop?

323
00:16:26,414 --> 00:16:29,614
Which privilege did we take away from the deployer, and why?

324
00:16:30,164 --> 00:16:32,575
Why does cleaning snapshots need two tasks?

325
00:16:33,125 --> 00:16:35,130
What are the two halves of a Nexus backup?

326
00:16:35,680 --> 00:16:37,749
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

327
00:16:48,299 --> 00:16:50,383
Objectives covered: immutability,

328
00:16:50,383 --> 00:16:51,961
retention and provenance,

329
00:16:51,961 --> 00:16:53,034
then permissions,

330
00:16:53,034 --> 00:16:54,486
backups and monitoring.

331
00:16:55,036 --> 00:16:55,990
Next: break it,

332
00:16:55,990 --> 00:16:56,435
fix it,

333
00:16:56,435 --> 00:16:57,007
prove it.

334
00:16:57,007 --> 00:16:58,215
Four real failures,

335
00:16:58,215 --> 00:16:59,868
read from the first error,

336
00:16:59,868 --> 00:17:01,457
and the mastery scenario.

337
00:17:02,007 --> 00:17:06,210
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss the next lesson.
